WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.037 --> 00:00:19.059
chilly March wind blew as Patrick suited up for the game at the Scorpions' field.

2
00:00:19.079 --> 00:00:20.600
"Hey, Todd!"

3
00:00:20.981 --> 00:00:23.783
He waved at the left fielder, who was warming up with the basemen.

4
00:00:24.883 --> 00:00:25.724
Todd grinned at him.

5
00:00:25.744 --> 00:00:27.245
"Hi, Patrick.

6
00:00:27.785 --> 00:00:29.947
I bet you're glad to see me back on the field!"

7
00:00:30.847 --> 00:00:31.528
Patrick nodded.

8
00:00:32.212 --> 00:00:34.834
"Left field is okay, but I'd much rather catch!"

9
00:00:34.854 --> 00:00:37.515
"Mickey did a good job filling in.

10
00:00:38.295 --> 00:00:40.757
He's a great addition to the team," Todd said.

11
00:00:41.937 --> 00:00:43.839
Patrick looked down, adjusting his pads.

12
00:00:43.859 --> 00:00:47.060
"Yeah," he said.

13
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.161
"See you later."

14
00:00:49.281 --> 00:00:51.443
He sprinted toward home plate to warm up with Dell.

15
00:00:51.463 --> 00:00:54.244
"Batter up!"

16
00:00:54.885 --> 00:00:56.545
called the umpire a few moments later.

17
00:00:57.846 --> 00:01:02.781
Patrick tried to settle into the familiar place behind the plate, but he felt like all eyes were

18
00:01:02.861 --> 00:01:03.181
on him.

19
00:01:05.262 --> 00:01:09.104
Todd wasn't the only one who'd said that Mickey had done a good job as catcher last week.

20
00:01:09.904 --> 00:01:13.425
Plus, Mickey was a fantastic hitter—much better than Patrick.

21
00:01:14.286 --> 00:01:16.427
"I can't mess up today," Patrick thought.

22
00:01:17.007 --> 00:01:20.789
He'd spent hours last night preparing for the game by reviewing players' stats.

23
00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:24.450
He probably should have spent just as much time on math.

24
00:01:25.331 --> 00:01:27.912
By the bottom of the seventh inning, the score was tied.

25
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.342
The first Scorpions batter stepped up and swung.

26
00:01:33.023 --> 00:01:34.344
Crack! A single.

27
00:01:36.726 --> 00:01:38.367
The next batter stepped into the box.

28
00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:42.049
Patrick flicked his gaze toward first base every few seconds.

29
00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:47.113
Dell couldn't easily see the runner, so it was up to Patrick to watch him closely.

30
00:01:48.254 --> 00:01:52.136
If the runner tried to steal a base, Patrick had to signal Dell quickly.

31
00:01:53.057 --> 00:01:57.380
Before Dell released the pitch, the runner started toward second, taking a long lead.

32
00:01:59.148 --> 00:02:00.889
Could Dell turn around and pick him off?

33
00:02:00.909 --> 00:02:04.251
"Not worth the risk," decided Patrick.

34
00:02:04.912 --> 00:02:07.294
He signaled for a curve ball, and Dell whipped it in.

35
00:02:07.314 --> 00:02:13.438
"Strike!" The runner dashed back to first base and took his lead again.

36
00:02:14.458 --> 00:02:17.861
Patrick could see Dell get ready for the pick, waiting for Patrick's signal.

37
00:02:18.701 --> 00:02:20.702
The runner inched closer toward second.

38
00:02:22.504 --> 00:02:23.824
He was really pushing it.

39
00:02:25.346 --> 00:02:28.808
Patrick considered trying to pick him off, but the runner was fast.

40
00:02:29.772 --> 00:02:33.034
Patrick knew that a mistake on the field would put the runner on second.

41
00:02:33.895 --> 00:02:37.097
So Patrick signaled the pitch, and Dell delivered the fastball.

42
00:02:38.619 --> 00:02:42.021
Whack! The ball rocketed far out to center field.

43
00:02:42.621 --> 00:02:45.664
The runner on first base rounded second and slid into third.

44
00:02:47.045 --> 00:02:49.386
By the end of the inning, the runner had scored.

45
00:02:50.087 --> 00:02:52.429
The Scorpions won the game, 5-4.

46
00:02:53.369 --> 00:02:56.872
Patrick was standing in the dugout with some other players when Daniel stalked over.

47
00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.041
"What's the matter with you, Patrick?"

48
00:03:01.421 --> 00:03:02.201
Daniel demanded.

49
00:03:02.221 --> 00:03:05.022
"That guy was an easy pick.

50
00:03:05.283 --> 00:03:06.443
You should have signaled Dell."

51
00:03:07.984 --> 00:03:09.284
Patrick kept his eyes down.

52
00:03:10.085 --> 00:03:10.885
Daniel was right.

53
00:03:11.525 --> 00:03:13.546
Patrick and Dell should have picked off the runner.

54
00:03:13.566 --> 00:03:18.228
"Everybody makes mistakes," Patrick said in a low voice.

55
00:03:19.609 --> 00:03:21.229
Mickey clapped Patrick on the shoulder.

56
00:03:21.249 --> 00:03:23.050
"Shake it off, dude.

57
00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:24.311
We'll win the next one."

58
00:03:25.351 --> 00:03:26.992
Patrick shrugged Mickey's hand away.

59
00:03:28.352 --> 00:03:30.613
"Players don't lose," Mickey went on.

60
00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:31.953
"Teams lose.

61
00:03:32.253 --> 00:03:33.173
It's not your fault."

62
00:03:33.193 --> 00:03:35.434
"Yes, it is my fault."

63
00:03:35.874 --> 00:03:37.054
Patrick glared at Mickey.

64
00:03:37.074 --> 00:03:39.475
"I was rusty today.

65
00:03:40.215 --> 00:03:42.416
That's from being stuck in left field last week."

66
00:03:43.796 --> 00:03:44.756
Dell looked surprised.

67
00:03:45.397 --> 00:03:46.217
"Hey, Patrick.

68
00:03:46.497 --> 00:03:47.197
Ease up a little."

69
00:03:48.297 --> 00:03:49.758
"Yeah," Daniel chimed in.

70
00:03:49.778 --> 00:03:50.798
"Knock it off."

71
00:03:51.958 --> 00:03:53.039
"It's been a bad day."

72
00:03:53.379 --> 00:03:54.039
Patrick sighed.

73
00:03:54.059 --> 00:03:56.420
"How about just giving me some space?"

74
00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.820
"Sure." Dell shook his head and walked away.

75
00:04:00.581 --> 00:04:02.901
Mickey and Daniel went to talk with some other players.

76
00:04:03.842 --> 00:04:07.943
Patrick felt Daniel's old mitt in his bag as he stuffed his other equipment inside.

77
00:04:07.963 --> 00:04:13.244
"This thing is more like an unlucky mitt," he thought.

78
00:04:14.445 --> 00:04:16.345
He should give it to Buff for a chew toy.

79
00:04:17.865 --> 00:04:20.086
Patrick avoided his friends at school the next morning.

80
00:04:20.866 --> 00:04:23.647
He felt embarrassed by the way he had acted after the game yesterday.

81
00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:26.728
"But it wasn't all your fault," he thought.

82
00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:29.942
Daniel shouldn't have criticized Patrick in front of everyone.

83
00:04:30.983 --> 00:04:33.185
He slid into a seat at an empty lunch table.

84
00:04:34.546 --> 00:04:38.709
He shoveled down the cafeteria's "Friday Special" and pulled out his math book.

85
00:04:41.432 --> 00:04:47.196
He had a test in 25—no, 24—minutes, and he definitely wasn't ready for it.

86
00:04:48.197 --> 00:04:49.458
He stared at his textbook.

87
00:04:50.159 --> 00:04:52.581
Did anyone really need to know about exponents?

88
00:04:52.601 --> 00:04:56.424
"I need to know or I'll flunk this test," he muttered.

89
00:04:57.432 --> 00:04:58.133
"You're kidding!"

90
00:04:58.713 --> 00:05:01.075
Dell's voice suddenly rose from a nearby table.

91
00:05:01.956 --> 00:05:02.996
He laughed loudly.

92
00:05:04.818 --> 00:05:05.979
Patrick looked to his left.

93
00:05:06.719 --> 00:05:10.162
Most of the members of the baseball team were gathered at the other table, talking to Coach.

94
00:05:11.103 --> 00:05:15.666
Mickey said something, and then Dell laughed loudly again, and motioned toward where Patrick sat.

95
00:05:16.807 --> 00:05:18.208
Patrick's face burned.

96
00:05:18.949 --> 00:05:20.069
Were they laughing at him?

97
00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.092
Maybe they were making fun of him because of his mom's van.

98
00:05:24.364 --> 00:05:27.927
Or, more likely, they were saying that his catching stunk yesterday.

99
00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:31.931
Patrick kept his eyes on his book and pretended he didn't hear them.

100
00:05:32.551 --> 00:05:33.512
But it wasn't easy.

101
00:05:34.533 --> 00:05:36.395
They kept talking and laughing loudly.

102
00:05:37.015 --> 00:05:40.759
Finally Patrick closed the book in frustration and put his head on top of it.

103
00:05:41.719 --> 00:05:42.340
"Test today?"

104
00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:43.781
asked a deep voice.

105
00:05:44.382 --> 00:05:45.383
Patrick raised his head.

106
00:05:46.063 --> 00:05:48.005
Coach loomed over him like a skyscraper.

107
00:05:48.025 --> 00:05:51.248
"I was just studying," Patrick said.

108
00:05:52.768 --> 00:05:55.170
Coach glanced at the closed book and raised his eyebrows.

109
00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:58.692
"I find it easier to study with my book open."

110
00:05:58.712 --> 00:06:00.773
"I'm just tired."

111
00:06:01.554 --> 00:06:03.395
Coach sat across the table from Patrick.

112
00:06:04.116 --> 00:06:05.937
"I heard you were having some problems in math."

113
00:06:05.957 --> 00:06:08.439
"What?" Patrick thought.

114
00:06:09.139 --> 00:06:10.740
Coach knew about his math grade?

115
00:06:11.801 --> 00:06:13.782
He glanced at the table where his teammates sat.

116
00:06:14.843 --> 00:06:18.285
Dell was the only one that Patrick had told about his last math quiz.

117
00:06:18.305 --> 00:06:21.187
"I can't believe Dell ratted on me!"

118
00:06:21.467 --> 00:06:22.088
Patrick thought.

119
00:06:23.032 --> 00:06:25.614
"The school has a tutoring program," continued Coach.

120
00:06:25.634 --> 00:06:28.655
"And strict rules about athletes' grades.

121
00:06:29.536 --> 00:06:31.837
So get some help sooner rather than later.

122
00:06:32.757 --> 00:06:36.600
Another failing quiz grade, Patrick, and you won't be able to play baseball."

123
00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.221
Patrick swallowed hard.

124
00:06:39.241 --> 00:06:41.422
"I'll do better, Coach.

125
00:06:41.562 --> 00:06:42.083
I promise."

126
00:06:43.023 --> 00:06:44.244
His face burned again.

127
00:06:45.224 --> 00:06:48.626
The whole team was watching as Coach lectured him about his crummy grades.

128
00:06:48.646 --> 00:06:53.013
"Good!" said Coach as the bell rang.

129
00:06:53.033 --> 00:06:54.754
"See you later at practice."

130
00:06:55.454 --> 00:06:56.855
"Yes, sir," said Patrick.

131
00:06:57.875 --> 00:06:59.916
Patrick ignored his teammates at the other table.

132
00:07:00.496 --> 00:07:02.978
He was still steaming over how they'd been talking about him.

133
00:07:04.278 --> 00:07:10.301
He broke out in a sweat, and suddenly he could feel the cafeteria's "Friday Special" rising in his

134
00:07:10.341 --> 00:07:14.484
stomach. He clamped a hand over his mouth and ran for the exit.
